{
  "decision": "test",
  "block": "signature_cases",
  "question": "Does {program} refuse an ECDSA P-256 signature when one of its two numbers (r or s) is zero or outside the allowed range?",
  "why": "Why it matters: a program that does not refuse such numbers may accept a signature that the key's owner never made, and the published note for these cases says such a signature can be forged without even knowing the message, so changed data could pass as properly signed.",
  "select": {
    "flags": ["InvalidSignature", "RangeCheck"],
    "ids": ["109", "151", "351", "363", "364", "376", "477"]
  },
  "control": "accept_all_verifier",
  "expect": "I expect the affected version to accept at least one of these must-reject signatures, most likely one where r and s are each zero or equal to the limit n (such as case 168, r=0 and s=0) and possibly one where a valid r is left unreduced above n (such as case 477), because it calculates with r and s without first refusing values outside 1 to n-1, and these cases point to that missing check and not to another fault because each is a plain pair of whole numbers with no encoding or type fault flagged in the file and with r or s at or beyond an edge of that range, which is why the wrong-type and damaged-encoding kinds are left out."
}

{
  "fix": "update",
  "version": "17.0.3",
  "quote": {"source": "S4", "text": "if (rb.signum() == 0 || sb.signum() == 0"},
  "summary": "Update OpenJDK 17.0.2 or earlier in the 17 line to 17.0.3, where the code that checks an ECDSA signature first tests the signature's two numbers (r and s) and returns false if either is zero or is not below the curve's fixed limit, a step the 17.0.2 code does not have; the sources name no fixed release for the 15 and 18 lines."
}

{
  "fix": "update",
  "version": "0.13.3",
  "quote": {"source": "S1", "text": "\"fixed\": \"0.13.3\""},
  "summary": "Update python-ecdsa to version 0.13.3, whose DER reader (used by its signature decoder) has checks that the 0.13.2 reader lacks: it raises an error when a number or a length is not written in its shortest form, for example with unneeded zero bytes, or when a number or sequence is stated to be longer than the data that is there."
}

{
  "fix": "update",
  "version": "0.13.3",
  "quote": {"source": "S1", "text": "They are part of the 0.13.3 release."},
  "summary": "Update python-ecdsa to version 0.13.3, where VerifyingKey.verify() and verify_digest() raise the documented BadSignatureError when the signature decoder reports a malformed signature, rather than letting a different error type through, and DER decoding refuses badly formatted numbers such as one padded with unnecessary zero bytes."
}
